AWS-010 — Workflow Compliance

Document ID: AWS-010
Title: Workflow Compliance
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Workflow Compliance Model (CWCM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Workflow Compliance Model establishes the principles, processes, evidence requirements, governance mechanisms, and reporting structures required to assess whether Workflow Definitions, Workflow Instances, and Workflow Programs satisfy applicable Atlas specifications, organizational policies, engineering standards, contractual obligations, and regulatory requirements.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Workflow compliance architecture
Compliance assessments
Compliance obligations
Compliance evidence
Continuous compliance
Exception handling
Compliance reporting
Compliance governance

This specification applies to every Workflow Definition, Workflow Instance, and Workflow Program.

3. Canonical Definition

Workflow Compliance is the demonstrable satisfaction of applicable governance, engineering, organizational, contractual, and regulatory obligations by workflow definitions and workflow executions, supported by objective, verifiable evidence.

Workflow Compliance evaluates workflow conformity.

It SHALL NOT modify workflow execution behavior.

4. Objectives

The Workflow Compliance Model SHALL:

Verify workflow conformity.
Preserve governance integrity.
Support certification.
Enable objective assessment.
Preserve evidence.
Support continuous monitoring.
Enable regulatory readiness.
Maintain traceability.
5. Compliance Principles

Every workflow compliance assessment SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Evidence-Based

Compliance SHALL be demonstrated through objective evidence.

Independence

Compliance assessments SHOULD remain independent of workflow authors and operators.

Repeatability

Equivalent assessments SHALL produce equivalent results.

Explainability

Compliance outcomes SHALL identify:

Applicable obligations.
Assessment results.
Supporting evidence.
Outstanding issues.
Traceability

Every assessment SHALL preserve complete traceability.

Continuous Assessment

Compliance SHALL be continuously reassessed throughout workflow operation.

6. Compliance Sources

Workflow compliance MAY be assessed against:

Source	Example
Atlas Specifications	ACC, AHS, ADS, APS, AWS
Organizational Policies	Internal governance
Engineering Standards	ISO, IEC, IEEE, BIM
Regulations	National and regional regulations
Contracts	Client obligations
Project Requirements	Project execution constraints

Multiple sources MAY apply simultaneously.

7. Compliance Assessment

Every assessment SHALL define:

Property	Description
Assessment ID	Unique identifier
Workflow Reference	Definition, Instance, or Program
Applicable Obligations	Evaluated requirements
Assessment Method	Manual, automated, or hybrid
Assessment Authority	Responsible assessor
Assessment Date	Evaluation timestamp
Assessment Outcome	Compliance result

Assessments SHALL be versioned.

8. Compliance Evidence

Every compliance assessment SHALL preserve:

Workflow definition version
Workflow execution history
Activity logs
State transitions
Policy decisions
Approval records
Compliance reports
Audit references
Supporting documents
Canonical Events

Evidence SHALL remain immutable.

9. Compliance Status

Atlas defines the following canonical workflow compliance states.

Status	Description
Compliant	All applicable obligations satisfied
Conditionally Compliant	Minor issues remain
Non-Compliant	Mandatory obligations violated
Exempted	Approved exception granted
Pending Assessment	Assessment not completed

Status SHALL remain explicitly recorded.

10. Continuous Compliance

Workflow Compliance SHALL support continuous monitoring.

Monitoring MAY detect:

Policy violations
Unauthorized workflow modifications
Missing approvals
Expired certifications
Regulatory changes
Activity deviations
Resource misuse
Security violations

Detected issues SHALL generate Canonical Events.

11. Exception Handling

Compliance exceptions SHALL define:

Exception identifier
Applicable obligation
Workflow reference
Justification
Risk assessment
Approval authority
Effective period
Mitigation measures

Exceptions SHALL conform to APS-011 — Policy Exceptions.

12. Compliance Reporting

Every compliance report SHALL include:

Workflow assessed
Assessment scope
Applicable obligations
Assessment outcome
Evidence summary
Exceptions
Outstanding findings
Recommendations

Reports SHALL be reproducible.

13. Compliance Governance

Every compliance assessment SHALL define:

Assessment authority
Review authority
Approval authority
Applicable governance domain
Applicable policies
Retention requirements

Governance SHALL remain independently auditable.

14. Compliance Quality

Every workflow compliance assessment SHALL satisfy:

Accuracy
Completeness
Objectivity
Traceability
Explainability
Repeatability
Auditability
Timeliness

Quality SHALL be continuously evaluated.

15. Compliance Events

Compliance activities MAY generate Canonical Events.

Examples include:

Compliance Assessment Started
Compliance Verified
Compliance Failed
Exception Granted
Compliance Restored
Compliance Expired

Events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

16. Conformance

A Workflow Compliance implementation conforms if it:

Performs governed compliance assessments.
Preserves immutable evidence.
Supports continuous monitoring.
Implements exception handling.
Produces standardized reports.
Maintains traceability.
Supports audit readiness.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted compliance verification.
Predictive workflow compliance.
Continuous regulatory synchronization.
Federated compliance assessment.
Machine-verifiable compliance proofs.
Autonomous compliance monitoring.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical workflow compliance semantics.

